[]
  ## Reproducing the Excel exercise
  \small
  Create an object called \inr{year} to hold the sequence of years. \\
```{r, year,size='scriptsize'}
year <- 1950:1961 # A vector of integers
year              # Type the name of an object to see its values
@
\pause
\vfill
  Note that you can use \inr{seq} to create sequences in a more
  general way. The \inr{c} function combines two or more vectors. \\
```{r, seq,size='scriptsize'}
updown <- c(seq(from=20, to=100, by=20), seq(from=100, to=20, by=-20))
updown            # Two sequences, combined
@   
\pause
\vfill
Use the \inr{length} function to determine the number of values in a
vector. \\
```{r, nYears,size='footnotesize'}
nYears <- length(year)
nYears
@



[]
  ## A simple population model
  Create an empty vector to store the data on females. Set female
  abundance to 100 in the first year.
```{r, females}
females <- rep(NA, nYears)
females[] <- 100
@
\pause
\vfill
Use a ``for loop'' to compute female abundance in subsequent years.
```{r, femaleLoop}
for(t in 2:nYears) {
    females[] <- females[] + females[]*0.01
}
@
\pause
\vfill
\centering
\alert{\bf We will use ``for loops'' for almost every population model
  that we implement in R} \\



[]
  ## A simple population model
  ::: columns
    ::: {.column width='50%'}
      Generate the data on males using a single line of code.
```{r, males,size='footnotesize'}
males <- females*0.8
@
    :::
\pause
    ::: {.column width='50%'}
      Put the objects in a \inr{data.frame}
```{r, data, size='scriptsize'}
model1 <- data.frame(year, females, males)
model1
@
    :::
  :::



[]
  ## Graphics
```{r, plot,size='tiny',fig.width=7,fig.height=4.6}
plot(females ~ year, data=model1, type="o", xlab="Year", ylab="Abundance",
     lwd=2, pch=16, ylim=c(0, 120))
lines(males ~ year, data=model1, type="o", col="blue", lwd=2, pch=16)
legend(x=1950, y=40, legend=c("Females", "Males"), col=c("black", "blue"), lty=1, pch=16)
@






  ## Assignment
  \small %\footnotesize
  
    * [] Create an Excel file and name it
      ``Yourlastname\_Yourfirstname''. 
    * [] Create the sheet shown on the next page using the
      techniques covered in this lab. 
      
        \footnotesize
        * Use auto-fill to create the first two columns.
        * For the ``Adults'' column, use the equation shown
          for cells C3 through C22. Note: For cell C2, you can
          directly enter the value ``10''.
      
    * [] Copy ``Sheet1'' to a new sheet and change the color and
      thickness of the lines. You can pick any colors and thicknesses
      you want. 
    * [] Replicate steps 1--3 using a ``for
      loop'' in a self-contained {R} script. Note that you will need
      code to create both graphs---the original one and the one with
      the new line color and thickness.  
      
      * [] Hint: You can make the juveniles data using \inr{seq}
        and \inr{c}, like we did with the \inr{updown} example earlier.  
      
    * [] Upload the Excel workbook (with both sheets) and the
      R script to ELC by 5:00pm on Friday.
  




